{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "title": "What this note does",
      "text": "%md\n## The online shop lakehouse, from a browser\n\nThis note reads the Delta Lake tables that `./mill examples.16-zeppelin-notebooks.run seed` wrote into\nthe MinIO object store, and turns them into numbers and charts. Nothing is installed on your machine:\nevery paragraph runs inside the Apache Zeppelin container.\n\n**Interpreters.** Zeppelin does not run code itself. It hands each paragraph to an *interpreter* - a\nseparate process that knows one language or one system. The first line of a paragraph, the *magic*,\npicks which one:\n\n| magic | interpreter setting | what it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `%md` | `md` | renders Markdown, like this paragraph |\n| `%spark` | `spark` | Scala against a live Apache Spark session |\n| `%spark.sql` | `spark`, its `sql` interpreter | SQL against the tables that session knows |\n| `%jdbc` | `jdbc` | SQL over a Java Database Connectivity driver; see note *02 Trino Federation* |\n\nThe three settings above are the only ones this stack configures, in\n`resources/interpreter/interpreter.json`. Open **Interpreter** in the top-right menu to read their\nproperties in the browser.\n\n**Run the paragraphs in order.** Zeppelin has no dependency graph between paragraphs: a paragraph sees\nwhatever the ones before it left behind in the interpreter process. The Scala paragraph below registers\nthe temporary views that every later `%spark.sql` paragraph queries, so it has to run first. Use\n*Run all paragraphs* (the play button in the note toolbar) and the order is taken care of.\n",
      "user": "anonymous",
      "config": {
        "editorSetting": {
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": true,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": false
        },
        "colWidth": 12.0,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "fontSize": 9.0,
        "editorHide": true,
        "title": true,
        "results": {},
        "enabled": true,
        "runOnSelectionChange": true,
        "checkEmpty": true
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "apps": [],
      "runtimeInfos": {},
      "progressUpdateIntervalMs": 500,
      "jobName": "paragraph_20250101-000001_1000",
      "id": "20250101-000001_1000",
      "dateCreated": "2025-01-01 00:00:00.000",
      "status": "READY"
    },
    {
      "title": "Read the Delta Lake tables",
      "text": "%spark\n// The Delta Lake tables live in MinIO, an object store that speaks the Amazon S3 protocol.\n// `s3a://` is Hadoop's S3 client, configured with the MinIO address and credentials in the\n// spark interpreter settings, so no keys appear in the notebook itself.\nval warehouse = \"s3a://lakehouse/shop\"\n\nval orders     = spark.read.format(\"delta\").load(s\"$warehouse/orders\")\nval orderLines = spark.read.format(\"delta\").load(s\"$warehouse/order_lines\")\n\n// A temporary view gives a DataFrame a name inside this Spark session. Every %spark.sql\n// paragraph below queries these names; they disappear when the interpreter is restarted.\norders.createOrReplaceTempView(\"orders\")\norderLines.createOrReplaceTempView(\"order_lines\")\n\nprintln(s\"orders:      ${orders.count()} rows\")\nprintln(s\"order_lines: ${orderLines.count()} rows\")\norders.printSchema()\norders.show(5, truncate = false)\n",
      "user": "anonymous",
      "config": {
        "editorSetting": {
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        },
        "colWidth": 12.0,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "fontSize": 9.0,
        "editorHide": false,
        "title": true,
        "results": {},
        "enabled": true,
        "runOnSelectionChange": true,
        "checkEmpty": true
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "apps": [],
      "runtimeInfos": {},
      "progressUpdateIntervalMs": 500,
      "jobName": "paragraph_20250101-000002_1001",
      "id": "20250101-000002_1001",
      "dateCreated": "2025-01-01 00:00:00.000",
      "status": "READY"
    },
    {
      "title": "Orders per day, for one country",
      "text": "%spark.sql\n-- ${country=DE,DE|PL|UA|FR|ES} is a Zeppelin *dynamic form*. It renders a dropdown above this\n-- paragraph, and the value you choose is substituted into the query before Spark sees it.\n-- Change the country and the paragraph re-runs on its own.\nSELECT\n  date_format(placed_at, 'yyyy-MM-dd') AS day,\n  count(*)                             AS orders,\n  round(sum(total_cents) / 100.0, 2)   AS revenue_eur\nFROM orders\nWHERE country = '${country=DE,DE|PL|UA|FR|ES}'\nGROUP BY date_format(placed_at, 'yyyy-MM-dd')\nORDER BY day\n",
      "user": "anonymous",
      "config": {
        "editorSetting": {
          "language": "sql",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        },
