    {
      "title": "Sharing, scheduling and what to try next",
      "text": "%md\n### Reading the numbers\n\nThe country dropdown and the article chart above answer the two questions a shop analyst asks first:\n*where are we selling* and *what are we selling*. Both came out of the same Delta Lake tables that the\nApache Spark and Apache Flink examples in this repository write, with no copy step in between - that is\nthe point of a lakehouse.\n\n### Sharing this note\n\n* **Export**: the note toolbar has an export icon that downloads the note as a `.zpln` file - the same\n  JSON format as `resources/notebooks/`. Committing that file to a repository is how a notebook becomes\n  reviewable work rather than a private scratchpad.\n* **Import**: *Import note* on the Zeppelin home page accepts such a file, or a URL to one.\n* **Link**: the address bar contains the note identifier (`2ZEPSHOP01`). Anyone with access to this\n  Zeppelin server can open the same note at that address.\n* **Schedule**: the clock icon in the toolbar accepts a cron expression, for example `0 0 6 * * ?` for\n  every morning at six. Zeppelin then runs every paragraph on that schedule and leaves the results in\n  the note, which turns it into a small daily report.\n\n### What to try next\n\n1. Add `PT` to the choices in the country dropdown and re-run: the query returns nothing, because the\n   shared data generator only produces the five countries listed.\n2. Change `%spark.sql` to `%jdbc` in one of the query paragraphs. It fails, because the temporary views\n   only exist inside the Spark session - note *02 Trino Federation* shows what `%jdbc` can see instead.\n",
      "user": "anonymous",
